IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `au-interpretation-time`

-- Acts Interpretation Act 1901 (Australia): PART 8, Distance, time and age (s 35).
-- The rest of Part 8 is in au-interpretation-time.l4: s 36 (calculating time), s 37
-- (expressions of time) and s 37A (attaining an age) all fix a DAY or a MOMENT.
-- LAYER 1: the law, objectively.

§ `Part 8: Distance`

§§ `Section 35: measurement of distance`

-- "In a straight line on a horizontal plane": the height of either point is not an input,
-- because the rule ignores it. The two points are given as east and north coordinates in
-- the same unit on a flat grid; the result is in that unit. The Act does not say how a
-- curved surface is to be flattened, so coordinates on a plane are the only input offered.
-- src:778 | 35 Measurement of distance
-- src:779 | In the measurement of any distance for the purposes of any Act, that distance shall be measured in a straight line on a horizontal plane.
@ref Acts Interpretation Act 1901 s 35 (src:778-779)
GIVEN `the east coordinate of the first point` IS A NUMBER
      `the north coordinate of the first point` IS A NUMBER
      `the east coordinate of the second point` IS A NUMBER
      `the north coordinate of the second point` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the distance for the purposes of an Act` `the east coordinate of the first point` `the north coordinate of the first point` `the east coordinate of the second point` `the north coordinate of the second point` MEANS
    SQRT ((eastDifference TIMES eastDifference) PLUS (northDifference TIMES northDifference))
    WHERE
        eastDifference MEANS `the east coordinate of the second point` MINUS `the east coordinate of the first point`
        northDifference MEANS `the north coordinate of the second point` MINUS `the north coordinate of the first point`
